# Python Backend Conventions

## FastAPI Structure

- Single app in `main.py` — one `/process` endpoint per workflow, no routers unless explicitly added
- All business logic lives in separate modules (`csv_parser.py`, `excel_writer.py`, `break_counter.py`) — `main.py` only orchestrates
- Each module exposes one primary function, clearly named, with typed inputs and outputs
- Use `pydantic` models for all request bodies — never raw `dict` from FastAPI
- Return `{ "success": true, ...data }` or `{ "success": false, "error": "plain English description" }` — no HTTP 4xx/5xx for business logic failures

## openpyxl Rules — critical, never violate

- Load with: `openpyxl.load_workbook(path)` — never `read_only=True` when writing
- Write values only: assign to `cell.value` — never touch `.font`, `.fill`, `.border`, `.alignment`, `.number_format`
- Find target row dynamically: iterate column B from row 4 downward, find first cell where `.value is None`
- Row 3 is template data — always start checking from row 4, never row 3
- Save with `wb.save(path)` after every write
- Never assume the file is open in Excel — but also never leave the file open after saving

## CSV Parsing

- Load with `pd.read_csv(filepath)`
- Strip whitespace from all string values before comparison: `str(val).strip()`
- Drop columns where header starts with `Unnamed`
- Raise a named exception class (`TagNotFoundError`) when no match is found — never return `None` silently
- Delete the CSV file after successful parse

## Date Handling

- i3 history dates are in format `MM-DD-YYYY HH:MM` — parse with `datetime.strptime(date_str, "%m-%d-%Y %H:%M")`
- Excel date values: write a `datetime.date` object directly — let openpyxl handle formatting
- Never write date strings to Excel cells

## Error Handling

- All file I/O wrapped in try/except with specific exception types — never bare `except:`
- Log warnings (not exceptions) for expected edge cases: no EP entry found, empty history, tag not found after retry
- FastAPI endpoint catches all exceptions and returns `{ "success": false, "error": str(e) }`

## Credentials

- Credentials live in Python process memory only — passed in on session start, never written to disk
- Never log credential values, even at debug level
